  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nTroughing pattern moves into the Ohio Valley region on Sunday, with a\n70mb cut off low and attendant weak surface low pressure slowly\nmoving through the region as we head into the working week. \nUnfortunately, it looks as though the very moist airmass sticks \naround at least through Sunday and into Monday before getting shunted\neastward. Diurnally driven convection is expected on Sunday, with \nscattered thunderstorms once again developing during the afternoon \nhours lingering into the evening. Can't rule out gusty winds with \ncollapsing storms, but given the airmass in place, high rain rates \nand heavy rainfall may pose more of a threat. With PWATs around 1.8 \nto 2 inches, very skinny CAPE, and high mid level RHs in forecast \nsoundings, expect torrential rain with popup storms that will lower \nvisibility and may lead to localized flooding concerns. \n\nAs the area of low pressure continues to work its way east, similar\nconcerns arise for Monday with diurnally driven convection in a very\nmoist airmass. PM thunderstorms are possible again with a limited\nsevere weather threat given lack of forcing and lack of robust\ninstability, but the heavy rain threat persists. \n\nWeak ridging moves into the region mid-week and we'll begin to dry\nout Tuesday afternoon. The next chance for precipitation looks to\narrive on Thursday, when another shortwave moves through the Great\nLakes. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2). \nAs the trough swings through the Ohio Valley and Great Lakes region,\nit will increase cloud cover across the area, inhibiting insolation.\nThis, combined with a shift to more northerly flow brings in some\nvery weak cold air advection. Daytime highs as we head into the work\nweek reach the low 80s Mon and Tues, with overnight lows falling into\nthe mid to upper 60s.",
